    "question": {
      "prompt": "Sign-ups rose from 2.1% to 4.6% of visitors. Which change most likely lifted conversion the most?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Cutting the form from nine fields to three",
          "correct": true,
          "explanation": "Yes. On a sign-up page the form **is** the conversion, and each field is a cost paid before any reward. Removing six — especially date of birth and full address — strips out the effort and privacy worries that make people abandon."
        },
        {
          "text": "Replacing three buttons with a single call to action",
          "explanation": "Focus helps: the old buttons led people away from the form. But visitors who did reach the form still faced nine fields — the bigger barrier sat there."
        },
        {
          "text": "Swapping the carousel for a static product hero",
          "explanation": "A static hero keeps the message on screen and loads faster, but on its own it seldom moves sign-ups as much as removing effort from the form."
        },
        {
          "text": "Adding the ratings and subscriber trust bar",
          "explanation": "Social proof reassures people at the moment of decision. It supports the change, but reassurance cannot make up for a form that asks too much."
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "In a real sprint you would test these changes separately (or in a multivariate test) to know each one's share of the lift."
    },
    "takeaway": "Good CRO removes friction before it adds persuasion: cut what you ask for, make one action obvious, then put proof right next to it."
